\begin{abstract}
LLM agent routing---deciding when to rely on internal reasoning versus external tool use---is typically addressed by training routers directly, without first understanding what mechanistic signal drives the routing preference. This absence of diagnostic methods risks building routers atop confounded or trivial signal. We propose an episode-level counterfactual framework that compares pure-strategy outcomes (internal-only vs.\ deterministic-only) to decompose routing signal into its mechanistic components before any router is constructed. Applying this framework to ScienceWorld (11 task types, 245 episodes), we find that 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels arise from death asymmetry---the internal strategy triggers catastrophic failure while the deterministic strategy survives. This finding is penalty-independent: a derived score-weighted measure, the Death Avoidance Factor, reaches 88--90\% across three model families (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B), confirming convergence on a single dominant mechanism. Cross-environment probes on MATH and ALFWorld diagnose qualitatively different routing mechanisms---no extractable signal and information asymmetry, respectively---demonstrating the framework's generality. A ${\sim}$50-episode diagnostic pilot suffices to identify the dominant routing mechanism in a new environment, enabling informed build-vs-buy decisions before expensive router training.
\end{abstract}

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool Use and External Computation in LLMs.}
A growing body of work augments language models with external tools \citep{tool-learning-survey-2024}, spanning several functional categories.
\emph{Self-supervised} approaches learn tool invocation from loss signals: Toolformer \cite{toolformer-2023} inserts API calls filtered by perplexity reduction; ToolACE \cite{toolace-2025} curates synthetic data for function-calling accuracy.
\emph{Token-level} methods embed tools into the model vocabulary: ToolkenGPT \cite{toolkengpt-2023} represents tools as special tokens resolved at inference.
\emph{Task-level} decomposition routes subtasks to specialized modules: ART \cite{art-2023} generates tool-augmented programs; Chameleon \cite{chameleon-2023} composes heterogeneous modules per subtask.
\emph{RL-based} methods learn routing policies from reward: ReTool \cite{retool-2025} discovers when code execution is preferable to chain-of-thought.
All focus on \emph{learning to use tools}; we ask a prior question: \emph{what signal drives the routing decision?}
Concurrent work examines \emph{when} to invoke tools: tool-call decisions correlate with uncertainty~\citep{tool-overuse-illusion-2026}, are encoded in hidden states~\citep{when2tool-2026}, impose a ``tool-use tax''~\citep{tool-use-tax-2026}, or follow necessity-utility-affordability logic~\citep{to-call-or-not-2026}. Our decomposition yields a concrete answer: 78.9\% of routing labels trace to catastrophic-failure prevention.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation and Routing.}
Adaptive routing traces back to learned halting policies \citep{graves2016act}. In the LLM era, CogRouter \cite{cogrouter-2026} adapts reasoning depth per step, ARES \cite{ares-2026} selects among reasoning effort levels, Mixture-of-Recursions \cite{mixture-of-recursions-2025} learns token-level recursion depths, and MTRouter \cite{mtrouter-2026} addresses multi-turn routing.
RouteLLM \citep{routellm-2024} learns routers from preference data to select between strong and weak models.
\citet{budget-aware-routing-2026} train episode-level routing policies under sparse terminal rewards---the closest setting to ours; they optimize \emph{which} model to invoke, whereas we diagnose \emph{what} drives the routing decision.

